<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qasim Amin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qasim Amin was an Egyptian jurist and Islamic modernist, a co-founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> University and often counted among the Arab world's first feminists. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, he took a law degree in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and studied at Montpellier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and from 1885 served in Egypt's courts. He criticised veiling, seclusion, early marriage and the lack of education for women, and his book The Liberation of Women, published in 1899, called for an end to the veil. His advocacy stirred debate across the Arab world, though scholars such as Leila Ahmed have questioned his feminism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qasim-amin.jpg" alt="Qasim Amin" width="260" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qasim_Amin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
